title: skg asks nothing of a writer beyond writing for himself, which is what Grudin found the successes had and the failures lacked.
pid: 3bbe5a83-82c2-40cb-abde-0b5d5c312155
body: |2-
  His failures: the meeting scheduler that needed everyone to keep a
  calendar for the convenor's sake; the project tracker that needed
  everyone to type for the manager. In skg a reader subscribes to,
  links to or forks a writer's notes "without either having to teach"
  (Tay's purpose 4), and the writer does nothing for it. Nor does skg
  need a critical mass, his second cause: purposes 1, 2, 3 and 5 serve
  a person alone, so the sharing rides on a tool already worth using.
  That is the first of his ways to proceed: add group features to a
  single-user application people already keep, rather than launch a
  group tool. skg's notes are Org files edited in Emacs or Neovim.
